{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791625391,"nonce":"CEVRq/KwZVYE5YQGdIn8lq9ZQXU=","title":"rayzor_anon_get_field reads an Array passed as a structure with methods","message":"Conformance row Issue2889. In a local generic\n`function mapMappable\u003cA,B\u003e(m:Mappable\u003cA\u003e, f:A-\u003eB) return m.map(f);` with\n`typedef Mappable\u003cY\u003e = { function map\u003cX\u003e(f:Y-\u003eX):Array\u003cX\u003e; }`, passing `[1]` makes\n`m.map` go through rayzor_anon_get_field on the Array object. The read walks garbage:\nwith one heap layout it SIGSEGVs at 0x0, with another it never terminates (the row\nmoved CRASH -\u003e TIMEOUT in the 80595cfa gate with no change to this path; batch 18\ncrashes at the same function). Under ASLR-off (gdb, setarch -R) the call reaches the\n`Mappable.map` trap stub instead.\n\n`var a:{ function map(f:Int-\u003eInt):Array\u003cInt\u003e; } = [1,2]; a.map(..)` in a plain\nfunction works, and so does filter. `var a:Dynamic = [1,2]; a.map(..)` SIGSEGVs.\n\nrayzor_anon_get_field should reject a receiver that is not an anon object instead of\nwalking it, and the structural call on an Array receiver needs a by-name dispatch to\nArray's methods.","files":null}]}